  "question": {
    "text": "<p>If one wants to remove all the mass of the earth to infinity in order to break it up completely. The amount of energy that needs to be supplied will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is ________.</p><p>(Round off to the Nearest Integer)</p><p>(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math>&nbsp;is the mass of earth, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> is the radius of earth,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>G</mi></math> is the gravitational constant)</p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "3",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Energy given&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p>"
